window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"About","u":"about","k":"Page","d":"Meet Nate Baird of Stoneward Construction: a licensed Utah contractor with a mortgage financing background and more than 4.5 million dollars in construction projects managed, building factory-built ADUs and small homes along the Wasatch Front.","h":"A builder who also understands the money. | Why I build with modular. | Numbers first. Straight answers. | Budget before plans | An honest read on your lot | Permits, site work and finish | One person to call | Licensed Utah contractor | Built indoors, set on your lot | Local project contact | Along the Wasatch Front. | Let's run the numbers on your lot. | Explore | Contact","b":"About Stoneward A builder who also understands the money. I'm Nate Baird, owner of Stoneward Construction. I manage more than 4.5 million dollars in construction projects, and I was a top 1% loan officer in 2022, so I understand both sides of the equation. Why I build with modular. Seeing a project from both sides changed how I look at it. It is not only what it costs to build, but what it appraises for, what it costs to carry, and what rent it can earn. When I ran the numbers on ADUs and small homes built the traditional way, they rarely worked. Stick-built came in too expensive, and the rent a small unit could bring in rarely came close to justifying the cost. Homeowners were being asked to take on a long, unpredictable build for a unit that struggled to pay for itself. Modular changed that math. Most of the work happens in a factory, so the time on your lot is shorter, there is less waste and weather risk, and the scope is defined in writing before the home is built. That is what makes an ADU or a small rental home a project you can actually plan around. So that is what Stoneward does. I help you decide whether a modular home makes sense on your lot, then handle the permits, site work and finish so you have one person to call from the first conversation to move-in. 4.5M+ dollars in construction projects managed R100 licensed Utah residential contractor Top 1% loan officer, 2"},{"t":"Free Utah ADU Guides and Worksheets","u":"adu-guides","k":"Guide","d":"Download free Utah ADU resources: a 10-step guide to building a backyard ADU, an income worksheet and a lot checklist, built around the SB 284 law.","h":"Plan your backyard ADU before you spend a dollar. | 10 Steps to Building a Backyard ADU in Utah | Backyard ADU Income Worksheet | Backyard ADU Lot Checklist | Rather have us check your lot? | Explore | Contact | Service area","b":"Free ADU guides Plan your backyard ADU before you spend a dollar. Three short downloads from a licensed Utah contractor: a step-by-step guide, an income worksheet and a lot checklist. Built around Utah's new detached ADU law, SB 284. Free download · 3 pages 10 Steps to Building a Backyard ADU in Utah What the new law allows, what a project really costs, and how a factory-built home gets from your lot to move-in, with starting prices. The SB 284 rules in plain language A whole-project budget, not just the home Financing, permits, crane day and finish work First name Email City Select your city American Fork Lehi Orem Saratoga Springs Mapleton Highland Provo Alpine Vineyard Pleasant Grove Lindon Sandy Taylorsville West Valley City Bountiful Layton West Point Another Utah city Phone (optional) Company Send me this download and a few helpful follow-up emails. I can unsubscribe any time. Send me the download Free download · 2 pages Backyard ADU Income Worksheet Run your own numbers. Rent, costs, loan payments and what is left over, with a worked example so you can see how the math goes. Project cost and yearly income, step by step Utah rules that affect rental income Try a lower rent and a higher cost First name Email City Select your city American Fork Lehi Orem Saratoga Springs Mapleton Highland Provo Alpine Vineyard Pleasant Grove Lindon Sandy Taylorsville West Valley City Bounti"},{"t":"ADU and Home Floor Plans","u":"adus","k":"Page","d":"Browse 33 factory-built ADU and home floor plans from Stoneward Construction, from 200 to 2,185 square feet. Filter by type, bedrooms and size, then request pricing.","h":"Find the plan that fits your lot. | Four finish series for every plan. | Designer Series | Modern Series | Rustic Series | Balance Series | Built to the same code as a site-built home. | Residential code, not HUD | Framed and insulated like a house | Inspected along the way | A real foundation | Not sure which plan is the right start? | Explore | Contact","b":"Floor plans Find the plan that fits your lot. Thirty-three plans in 17 series, from a 200 sq ft studio to a 2,185 sq ft family home. Each is available in four finish series . Every plan shows a starting price, and ADU plans start at the price shown on each plan . Your written contract confirms the final number. Hover a photo, or tap Floor plan, to see the layout. Type All ADUs Homes Bedrooms All 1 bed 2 bed 3+ bed Size All Under 600 sq ft 600 to 1,000 sq ft Over 1,000 sq ft All 33 plans No plans match those filters. Try a wider size range or another bedroom count. Renderings may show optional upgrades. Availability, pricing and delivery are confirmed in your written quote. Bedroom filters show plans available with that bedroom count. Please enable JavaScript to browse and filter the plans, or contact Stoneward for the current plan list. Finish packages Four finish series for every plan. Every plan comes in four curated style packages. Each comes with a set of standard features and the option to add upgrades, so the same floor plan can look and feel very different. Designer Series Smart design Your choices Your values See standard features and upgrades Modern Series Modern design Clean lines Streamlined profiles See standard features and upgrades Rustic Series Mountain design Natural finishes Rustic charm See standard features and upgrades Balance Series Transitional design High"},{"t":"Alpine ADUs: Adopted Rules, 1,200 sq ft Cap","u":"alpine-adu","k":"City","d":"Alpine adopted detached ADU rules in Sept. 2026 with a reported 1,200 sq ft cap. See what is confirmed and plans for larger Alpine lots.","h":"Alpine has adopted detached ADU rules. | What we confirmed for Alpine | Lone Peak above, big lots below, and a rule still being tuned. | What this means for a Alpine homeowner. | A 1,200 sq ft cap | Lot coverage is next | Registration | Larger lots | The biggest plans that still fit a 1,200 sq ft cap. | Long runs and mountain-side access. | From a Alpine lot to move-in. | Alpine ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Alpine?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Alpine Alpine, Utah County Alpine has adopted detached ADU rules. The Alpine City Council adopted its detached ADU ordinance in early September 2026 to meet SB 284. If you own a larger Alpine lot, a separate home in the backyard may now be possible, within the city's size cap. Adopted September 2026 Check my Alpine lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Alpine Source: Alpine City Council and Planning Commission coverage, September 2026; Deseret News, October 14, 2026; Utah Code 10-21-304. Adopted September 2026, after extended council debate Size Maximum ADU size of 1,200 sq ft, as reported Registration Annual ADU registration was recommended by the Planning Commission Next step The council said it will revisit lot-coverage rules within six months State floor Lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home must qualify Parking State law caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Not confirmed: The ordinance number and vote count, plus final setbacks, parking and lot-coverage rules. Confirm with Alpine City. About Alpine Lone Peak above, big lots below, and a rule still being tuned. Alpine sits at the northeast corner of Utah County at the foot of Lone Peak, with Highland and Lehi to the west and the mouth of American Fork Canyon nearby. It is a city of large lots, mountain views and long driveways, and the quarter-acre t"},{"t":"American Fork ADUs: Backyard Homes Now Allowed","u":"american-fork-adu","k":"City","d":"American Fork adopted detached ADU rules Sept. 22, 2026: 1,500 sq ft footprint, 24 ft height, 12 ft setbacks. See what fits and which modular plans work.","h":"Backyard ADUs are now allowed in American Fork. | What we confirmed for American Fork | A canyon-mouth city with a long history of backyard sheds. | What this means for a American Fork homeowner. | Family close by | Long-term rental income | The 25 percent rule | Short disruption | Plans sized for the 25 percent yard rule. | What American Fork will check before it says yes. | From a American Fork lot to move-in. | American Fork ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in American Fork?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / American Fork American Fork, Utah County Backyard ADUs are now allowed in American Fork. American Fork adopted detached ADU rules on September 22, 2026, to meet Utah's new SB 284. If you own a lot of 11,000 square feet or more, a factory-built home in the backyard may now be an option. Stoneward is based right here in American Fork. Adopted September 22, 2026, 4 to 0 Check my American Fork lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for American Fork Source: American Fork City Council action of September 22, 2026, as reported by the American Fork Citizen on September 29, 2026; Utah Code 10-21-304. Lot At least 11,000 sq ft, with an existing detached single-family home Footprint Up to 1,500 sq ft or the main home's footprint, whichever is smaller Height No taller than the main house, and no more than 24 ft Setbacks 12 ft on most sides and rear, 20 ft on the street side of a corner lot Yard coverage ADU plus other accessory buildings: 25% of the rear yard open space at most Parking 1 space under 650 sq ft, 2 spaces at 650 sq ft or more Occupancy Owner lives in the main home or the ADU, one ADU per lot Rentals 90 consecutive days or longer Not confirmed: The council said it will revisit parking, height and impact fees, so confirm the current text with American Fork City before you finalize a design. About American Fork A canyon-mouth city with a long history"},{"t":"Bountiful ADUs: Administrative Approval","u":"bountiful-adu","k":"City","d":"Bountiful moved detached ADUs from conditional use to administrative approval on Sept. 22, 2026. See what changed and plans for bench lots.","h":"Bountiful moved detached ADUs off conditional use. | What we confirmed for Bountiful | Bench-area lots and a one-step process. | What this means for a Bountiful homeowner. | No discretionary hearing | Objective standards | Standards cover the basics | Davis County option | Compact plans for a bench-area yard. | Slope, access and old lines on a mature lot. | From a Bountiful lot to move-in. | Bountiful ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Bountiful?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Bountiful Bountiful, Davis County Bountiful moved detached ADUs off conditional use. Bountiful used to treat detached ADUs as conditional uses. On September 22, 2026 the City Council adopted Ordinance 2026-08, creating an administrative approval process with objective development standards, as SB 284 requires. Ordinance 2026-08 adopted, September 22, 2026 Check my Bountiful lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Bountiful Source: Bountiful City Ordinance No. 2026-08; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before Detached ADUs were conditional uses Now An administrative approval process with objective standards Adopted September 22, 2026, Ordinance 2026-08 Planning Commission Recommended approval 7 to 0 on September 15 Standards The code addresses lot size, unit size, owner occupancy, parking, height and setbacks State law No conditional use permit in a primarily residential zone Not confirmed: The specific lot-size, size, height, setback and parking figures in Bountiful's adopted standards. Confirm them with Bountiful Planning. About Bountiful Bench-area lots and a one-step process. Bountiful sits in Davis County between Salt Lake City and Farmington, with older neighborhoods on the benches below the Wasatch Front and newer streets toward the valley. Mueller Park and the mountain trails are close, and many homes have mature yards and some slope. Bountiful is a rar"},{"t":"Contact","u":"contact","k":"Page","d":"Talk with Stoneward Construction about a modular ADU or small modular home. Tell us about your lot and the plan you have in mind.","h":"Let's talk about your ADU. | Request a plan and pricing | Talk to a person | What to include | Start with the floor plans. | Explore | Contact | Service area","b":"Contact Stoneward Let's talk about your ADU. Tell us where you are and what you have in mind. We'll get back to you soon. Request a plan and pricing Name Phone Email City Lot situation Choose one I own a lot with a spot in mind I own a lot and need help siting the unit I am still looking for a lot Not sure yet Plan of interest Not sure yet Message Create email request Call Stoneward This form opens an email draft in your email app with the details above. Nothing is sent until you press send in your email app. Talk to a person Phone Email Office Service area Call Stoneward Email Stoneward We'll get back to you soon. What to include Your city or nearest cross streets Whether you own the lot or are still looking A plan name from the ADUs page, if one caught your eye Any questions about the process or your lot Prefer email? Write to Stoneward directly and include the same details. Start with the floor plans. Look at the floor plans first, then tell us which ones you want to talk about. See floor plans How it works"},{"t":"ADU and Modular Home FAQ","u":"faq","k":"Guide","d":"Straight answers on modular ADU cost, permits in Utah, financing, timeline, warranty and what the price includes, from Stoneward Construction.","h":"Honest answers, before you start. | The basics | Cost and budget | Timeline and process | Your lot, permits and approvals | Financing | Quality and warranty | Still have a question? | Explore | Contact | Service area","b":"Questions Honest answers, before you start. Permits, pricing, financing, timelines, warranty. Here is what homeowners ask first. If your question is not here, call or write and you will get a straight answer. The basics Cost and budget Timeline and process Your lot, permits and approvals Financing Quality and warranty The basics What is a modular home or ADU? A modular home is built in sections, called modules, inside a factory. The modules are trucked to your lot, set on a permanent foundation by crane and finished on site. An ADU (accessory dwelling unit) is a small, separate home on the same lot as a main house: a backyard cottage, guest house, in-law suite or rental unit. Is modular the same as a mobile or manufactured home? No. Manufactured and mobile homes are built to the federal HUD code on a steel chassis. Modular homes are built to the same International Residential Code (IRC) your city applies to a site-built house, and they are set on a permanent foundation. That is why they are generally treated as real property and can be appraised and financed much like a home built on site. Your lender and your city make the final call on your project. How is it different from a stick-built house? The finished product is a regular house. The difference is where and how it is built. Most of the construction happens indoors on a production schedule, so materials stay out of the we"},{"t":"Finish Series and Options","u":"finishes","k":"Page","d":"See what comes standard and what can be upgraded in the Designer, Modern, Rustic and Balance finish series: siding, roofing, windows, cabinets, counters, flooring and fixtures.","h":"Four finish series. Pick your look. | Designer Series | Exteriors | Electrical/lighting | Plumbing | Interior finishes | Appliances | Modern Series | Exteriors | Electrical/lighting | Plumbing | Interior finishes | Appliances | Rustic Series","b":"Finish series Four finish series. Pick your look. Every floor plan can be built in any of four finish series. Each one sets what comes standard, from siding and roofing to cabinets, counters and fixtures, and shows the upgrades you can add. Choose a series to see its options. Designer Series Modern Series Rustic Series Balance Series Designer Series Smart design · Your choices · Your values Exteriors Electrical/lighting Plumbing Interior finishes Appliances Exteriors Siding Standard Horizontal siding Textured Lap Siding Throughout - Painted 2 Sherwin Williams Color (Trim & Body) Upgrade Board and batten Textured B&B Siding Throughout - Painted 1 Sherwin Williams Color Upgrade Board and batten Plywood Sheets with Wood Battens Over Top at 16\" Spacing. Exterior paint Snowbound Gossamer Veil Felted Wool Urbane Bronze Iron Ore Passive Tin Lizzie Gibraltar Web Gray Cyberspace **We recommend making color selections from physical Sherwin Williams paint swatches** Roof material Standard Composite shingle Lifetime Architectural Asphalt Composite Shingles Weathered Wood Charcoal Grey Dual Grey Dual Brown Dual black Soffit/fascia Standard Open Eaves Painted to Match Trim Upgrade Aluminum Soffit/Fascia Exterior decking Standard Trex Enhance with Exposed Fasteners Upgrade Trex Enhance with Concealed Fasteners Saddle Clam Shell Toasted Sand Coastal Bluff Beach dune Rocky harbor Foggy warf Ext"},{"t":"Gallery","u":"gallery","k":"Page","d":"Photos of completed factory-built modular homes and renderings of every Stoneward ADU and small home floor plan in Utah.","h":"Factory-built homes, finished. | What factory-built looks like on site. | Renderings of every plan. | See one you like? | Explore | Contact | Service area","b":"Gallery Factory-built homes, finished. A look at completed modular homes and at the floor plans you can choose from. Tap any photo to enlarge it. Completed homes What factory-built looks like on site. Floor plans Renderings of every plan. Renderings may show optional upgrades. Open any plan for photos, the floor plan and details. All floor plans See one you like? Tell us which plan or look you are drawn to and we will talk through whether it fits your lot and budget. Start your ADU project See floor plans"},{"t":"Highland ADUs: Ordinance O-2026-28","u":"highland-adu","k":"City","d":"Highland adopted Ordinance O-2026-28 on Sept. 1, 2026: one ADU per property, owner occupancy on title. See plans that fit Highland lots.","h":"Highland has adopted its detached ADU rules. | What we confirmed for Highland | Foothill lots, long driveways and a title-recorded rule. | What this means for a Highland homeowner. | One ADU per property | Owner occupancy on title | Building and fire codes apply | Separate detached rules | Plans for a larger Highland lot. | Grading and long runs on foothill lots. | From a Highland lot to move-in. | Highland ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Highland?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Highland Highland, Utah County Highland has adopted its detached ADU rules. Highland City adopted its detached ADU ordinance on September 1, 2026, ahead of the state's October 1 deadline. If you own a larger Highland lot, a factory-built home in the backyard may now be on the table. Ordinance O-2026-28 adopted, September 1, 2026 Check my Highland lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Highland Source: Highland City legal notice of adoption, September 2026; Highland City Council coverage; Utah Code 10-21-304. Adopted September 1, 2026, Ordinance O-2026-28 Council vote 5 to 0, as reported Per property One ADU, internal or detached Owner occupancy Recorded against the property title Codes Building and fire code compliance required Detached units Separate size, setback and height limits apply Not confirmed: The adopted size cap, setbacks and height for detached units. The planning commission proposed caps in July, and the final numbers may differ. Confirm with Highland Planning. About Highland Foothill lots, long driveways and a title-recorded rule. Highland sits on the east side of Utah Valley between American Fork and Alpine, along the foothills with mountain views and the Alpine Highway running through it. Many residential lots here can be larger than the quarter-acre state threshold, so the rule may reach a good share of homeowners. Highland's or"},{"t":"How It Works","u":"how-it-works","k":"Guide","d":"See the typical process for a modular ADU with Stoneward Construction, from design and approvals through factory build, delivery and finish work.","h":"From first call to move-in. | Talk it through | Pick a plan | Feasibility on your lot and approvals | Factory build and delivery | Set and finish | The factory's eight milestones. | Feasibility review | Preconstruction | Site visit | Factory construction | Four-way walk | Pre-wrap walk | Delivery and set","b":"How it works From first call to move-in. This is how a typical project runs. Foundation and site work run alongside the factory build, and a project manager is assigned to your project. Set day 01 Talk it through Tell us about your lot, your household and how you want the ADU to work. Is it a guest house, a home office, a place for family or a small primary home? We'll talk through access, utilities and the practical questions early, before you spend on design. 02 Pick a plan Choose from the 33 plans we sell, from backyard studios to family homes. Each is available in several finishes. We'll compare layouts with your lot and how you want to use the space, then confirm the plan and finish direction. 03 Feasibility on your lot and approvals We review your lot for access, setbacks, utilities, slope and local requirements. The city or county makes the approval decision, and we do not promise an outcome. We coordinate the application with you and the factory and tell you what we see before you commit. State approvals for the modular design are handled by the factory as part of its process. 04 Factory build and delivery While foundation and site work happen on your lot, your modules are built indoors at a Utah factory. When the modules and your site are ready, they ship to you. A project manager is assigned to your project. 05 Set and finish Modules are set on your lot and the finish"},{"t":"Stoneward Construction | Modular ADUs in Utah","u":"","k":"Page","d":"Stoneward Construction is a licensed Utah residential contractor building factory-built modular ADUs and small homes along the Wasatch Front, with one local contact from first call to move-in.","h":"Modular ADUs, built for Utah. | Where backyard ADUs are now possible. | A calmer way to add a home. | The crew backlog | Cost creep on stick-built | Weather delays | Managing permits and trades | Three ways an ADU can work. | Backyard ADU | Casita or guest house | Small modular home | Five steps, one local contact. | Talk it through | Pick a plan","b":"Licensed Utah contractor Modular ADUs, built for Utah. Factory-built ADUs and small homes, built indoors on a schedule you can plan around. One local contractor from your first call to move-in. Start your ADU project → See floor plans → Is your city covered? → Serving The Bunkhouse New Utah law Since October 1, 2026, larger cities must allow a detached ADU on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more. What SB 284 means Find your city Cities near you Where backyard ADUs are now possible. Rules differ from city to city. Pick yours to see what it allows, what we confirmed and which plans fit. Compare all cities American Fork Orem Saratoga Springs Lehi Mapleton Highland Provo Alpine Vineyard Pleasant Grove Lindon Sandy Taylorsville West Valley City Bountiful Layton West Point Why a modular ADU A calmer way to add a home. Stick-built can work. It also puts every trade, permit and weather day on your lot. A modular build moves the house into a factory and keeps the site work moving alongside it. The crew backlog A site build waits its turn for framers, electricians and plumbers. With modular, much of the construction happens inside a factory, where the work is part of a production schedule. Cost creep on stick-built When a home is built in the open, changes and surprises can add up. A factory plan and a written scope give you a clearer picture before construction starts. Your quote confirms prici"},{"t":"Layton ADUs: Detached Rules and SB 284 Update","u":"layton-adu","k":"City","d":"Layton has allowed detached ADUs since 2021 and updated its standards with Ordinance 26-27 in September 2026. See what we confirmed, what to check, and plans that fit Layton lots.","h":"Layton already allowed detached ADUs, and updated the rules. | What we confirmed for Layton | Davis County's biggest city, with a head start. | What this means for a Layton homeowner. | Not starting from scratch | A permit is required | Standards just updated | Davis County choice | Plans that suit a Layton backyard. | Flat lots, long utility runs and a clear approach. | From a Layton lot to move-in. | Layton ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Layton?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Layton Layton, Davis County Layton already allowed detached ADUs, and updated the rules. Unlike many Utah cities, Layton did not have to start from zero. It adopted standards for internal, attached and detached ADUs in 2021. On September 17, 2026 the City Council adopted Ordinance 26-27 to line up with SB 284, so the lot-size and size details are worth confirming. Ordinance 26-27 adopted, September 17, 2026 Check my Layton lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Layton Source: Layton City Ordinance 21-23; Layton City ADU handout (May 2026); Layton City Council meeting of September 17, 2026 (Ordinance 26-27), as published on OpenUtah and Utah ADU Builder; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before Ordinance 21-23, adopted September 16, 2021, set standards for internal, attached and detached ADUs City guidance A permit is required, and the full rules are in Section 19.06.300 (May 2026 handout) State requirement Allow a detached ADU on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home Amendment Ordinance 26-27, City Council, September 17, 2026: drops matching-materials, window, roof-pitch and entrance rules, adds a conversion process and simplifies the setback and height table, per the published council record Parking State law caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Lots Detached ADUs remain allowed on lots of 6,000 sq ft or more, per th"},{"t":"Lehi ADU Rules: 11,000 sq ft Lot Minimum","u":"lehi-adu","k":"City","d":"Lehi cut its detached ADU lot minimum from 14,520 to 11,000 sq ft in July 2026. See the 6 ft spacing rule and narrow plans that fit.","h":"Lehi lowered its lot size for backyard ADUs. | What we confirmed for Lehi | Tech corridor, old Main Street, and a band of in-between lots. | What this means for a Lehi homeowner. | From 14,520 to 11,000 | Six feet from the house | One per lot | Close to where people work | Narrow plans for the newly eligible lots. | Routing utilities on a mid-size Lehi lot. | From a Lehi lot to move-in. | Lehi ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Lehi?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Lehi Lehi, Utah County Lehi lowered its lot size for backyard ADUs. Lehi already allowed detached ADUs, but only on lots of 14,520 square feet or more. The city updated its code this summer, and the minimum is now the state's 11,000 square feet, which brings in a lot of new homeowners. Rules updated, July 2026 Check my Lehi lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Lehi Source: Deseret News, October 14, 2026; Herald Extra, July 15, 2026; Lehi City detached ADU guidance. Before Detached ADUs only on lots of 14,520 sq ft (a third of an acre) or more Now Lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home Spacing A detached unit must be at least 6 ft from the main house Limit One ADU per lot Eligible lots Lehi's development director estimated eligible lots could grow by 20 percent or more Adopted July 2026 Not confirmed: Lehi's final size, setback and parking figures for each lot size. Confirm them with Lehi Planning. About Lehi Tech corridor, old Main Street, and a band of in-between lots. Lehi anchors the north end of Utah Valley. The I-15 corridor that people call Silicon Slopes runs through it, Thanksgiving Point is a regional draw, and the historic downtown sits well apart from the newer hillside and lakeside subdivisions. Between the older neighborhoods and the new ones are plenty of quarter-acre lots. That is where Lehi's change lands. A lot "},{"t":"Lindon ADUs: 12,000 sq ft Lots and SB 284","u":"lindon-adu","k":"City","d":"Lindon's detached ADU rule asked for 12,000 sq ft lots; SB 284 caps it at 11,000. See Ordinance 2026-16-O, what the commission changed, and plans for Lindon lots.","h":"Lindon is lowering its detached ADU lot minimum. | What we confirmed for Lindon | A small city between two bigger neighbors. | What this means for a Lindon homeowner. | 12,000 down to 11,000 | No five-year residency rule | Separate sewer struck | Parking relief | Compact plans for lots near the new minimum. | Sewer and water: the cost that was on the table. | From a Lindon lot to move-in. | Lindon ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Lindon?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Lindon Lindon, Utah County Lindon is lowering its detached ADU lot minimum. Lindon already allowed detached ADUs, but city staff said its 12,000 square foot minimum had to come down to match SB 284's 11,000. An ordinance implementing the state law was before the City Council on September 21, 2026, and Deseret News lists Lindon among the cities that adopted compliant rules. Ordinance 2026-16-O before the council September 21, 2026 Check my Lindon lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Lindon Source: Lindon City Council staff report, September 2026; local coverage of the Lindon Planning Commission; Deseret News, October 14, 2026; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before Detached ADUs on lots of at least 12,000 sq ft, per city staff and a Utah County guide State ceiling A city cannot require more than 11,000 sq ft, so the 12,000 sq ft minimum has to be reduced Ordinance Ordinance 2026-16-O, implementing SB 284, was before the council on September 21, 2026 Planning Commission Recommended approval in mid-September, with edits on owner occupancy and sewer hookups Parking State law caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Reported Deseret News lists Lindon among cities adopting compliant ordinances Not confirmed: The adopted text and effective date. The staff report left the final adoption date blank, so confirm Ordinance 2026-16-O with Lindon C"},{"t":"Mapleton ADU Rules: Half Acre to 11,000 sq ft","u":"mapleton-adu","k":"City","d":"Mapleton's old code required a half acre for a detached ADU. See how SB 284 caps it at 11,000 sq ft and which plans suit larger lots.","h":"Mapleton: from half an acre toward a quarter acre. | What we confirmed for Mapleton | A foothill city built on bigger lots. | What this means for a Mapleton homeowner. | Half an acre is the old bar | Size scales with the lot | Ask about your subdivision | Room for a real second home | Plans that use a larger Mapleton lot. | Longer runs and no-sewer questions on larger lots. | From a Mapleton lot to move-in. | Mapleton ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Mapleton?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Mapleton Mapleton, Utah County Mapleton: from half an acre toward a quarter acre. Mapleton's earlier code required 21,780 square feet, a half acre, for a detached accessory apartment. The City Council voted this year to amend its ADU code to comply with state law, which caps the lot size a city can require at 11,000 square feet. ADU code amended in 2026 Check my Mapleton lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Mapleton Source: Mapleton City Code (pre-amendment); local council coverage; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before Detached accessory apartments needed a lot of 21,780 sq ft (half an acre) State ceiling A city cannot require more than 11,000 sq ft for a detached ADU Council action Voted unanimously to amend the ADU code and keep its size limits by lot size Special areas The Harvest Park subdivision keeps an existing exemption Removed A 40 percent limit on ADU size Not confirmed: The exact lot-size threshold in Mapleton's adopted code and its size caps by lot size. Confirm both with Mapleton before you spend money on plans. About Mapleton A foothill city built on bigger lots. Mapleton sits at the southeast edge of Utah Valley, under Maple Mountain, with Springville and Spanish Fork as neighbors. The city has a semi-rural character, with larger lots than most of its neighbors and a history of orchards and open ground. That is why its old half-acre rule "},{"t":"Orem Detached ADU Rules: One-Story Limit","u":"orem-adu","k":"City","d":"Orem never allowed detached ADUs until SB 284. See the adopted one-story, 20 ft rule and which compact modular plans suit an Orem backyard.","h":"Backyard ADUs are now allowed in Orem. | What we confirmed for Orem | Between the lake and the mountain, one story at a time. | What this means for a Orem homeowner. | A first for Orem | One story, 20 feet | A big university next door | Plan the yard first | Single-story plans for a 20 foot limit. | Utilities and meters in an Orem backyard. | From a Orem lot to move-in. | Orem ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Orem?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Orem Orem, Utah County Backyard ADUs are now allowed in Orem. Orem had never allowed detached accessory dwelling units before Utah's SB 284. The city adopted rules this fall to meet the October 1 state deadline, so owners of qualifying lots can plan a separate home in the backyard, as long as it stays at one story. Rules adopted, September 2026 Check my Orem lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Orem Source: Deseret News, October 14, 2026; Orem City Council agenda for September 22, 2026; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before Detached ADUs were not allowed in Orem Now Allowed on qualifying single-family lots under the city's new rules State floor Lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home must qualify Height Single story, 20 ft maximum, as reported in October 2026 Parking State law caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Rentals Cities may require rentals of 90 consecutive days or longer Not confirmed: Orem's final lot-size wording, unit size caps, setbacks and owner-occupancy details. Confirm them with Orem Planning before you design. About Orem Between the lake and the mountain, one story at a time. Orem sits in the middle of Utah Valley, with Mount Timpanogos to the east and Utah Lake to the west. State Street and University Parkway carry the city's traffic, and Utah Valley University anchors the center of town. Many of"},{"t":"Pleasant Grove ADUs: New Parking Rule for Small Units","u":"pleasant-grove-adu","k":"City","d":"Pleasant Grove reportedly cut parking for detached ADUs under 650 sq ft to one added space on Oct. 7, 2026. See what is confirmed, what is not, and plans under 650 sq ft.","h":"Pleasant Grove just cut parking for small backyard ADUs. | What we confirmed for Pleasant Grove | Under Timpanogos, with a parking rule that favors small plans. | What this means for a Pleasant Grove homeowner. | Under 650 means one space | Accessory apartments already allowed | Smaller plans win | Confirm the rest | Plans under 650 square feet. | Where the parking space actually goes. | From a Pleasant Grove lot to move-in. | Pleasant Grove ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Pleasant Grove?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Pleasant Grove Pleasant Grove, Utah County Pleasant Grove just cut parking for small backyard ADUs. Pleasant Grove's 2016 ordinance already allowed an accessory apartment to be attached or detached. On October 7, 2026 the council reportedly adopted a parking change for SB 284, requiring one added space instead of two for a detached unit under 650 square feet. Parking change adopted October 7, 2026 Check my Pleasant Grove lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Pleasant Grove Source: Local coverage of the Pleasant Grove City Council, October 2026; Pleasant Grove moderate income housing strategy; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before The city's 2016 ordinance allowed an accessory apartment to be attached or detached Parking change Ordinance 2026-30, reportedly adopted unanimously October 7, 2026 Rule One added off-street space, instead of two, for a detached unit under 650 sq ft State floor Lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home must qualify Larger units Parking above 650 sq ft is still up to two spaces, the state cap Status Lot-size, size and owner-occupancy rules not confirmed Not confirmed: Pleasant Grove's lot-size minimum, unit size caps and owner-occupancy rules for detached ADUs. Confirm Ordinance 2026-30 and the city's accessory apartment code with Pleasant Grove. About Pleasant Grove Under Timpanogos, with a parking rule that favors s"},{"t":"Provo ADUs: Parking Cut, 25 ft Draft Height","u":"provo-adu","k":"City","d":"Provo is rewriting its ADU rules for SB 284: 11,000 sq ft lots, parking cut from four spaces to one or two. See where it stands.","h":"Provo is moving to open its backyards. | What we confirmed for Provo | A college town where most households rent. | What this means for a Provo homeowner. | Foothills and west side | From four spaces to two | A taller draft limit | Draft, not final | A 25 foot draft limit opens two-story plans. | Foothill and west-side lots are different projects. | From a Provo lot to move-in. | Provo ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Provo?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Provo Provo, Utah County Provo is moving to open its backyards. Provo's rules asked for at least four off-street parking spaces for a house and its ADU combined, which made detached units hard. Under SB 284 that has to change, and the city has a draft that allows detached ADUs on lots of 11,000 square feet or more. Planning Commission backed it October 14, 2026; council vote expected Check my Provo lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Provo Source: Deseret News, October 14, 2026; local reporting on the Provo Planning Commission; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before At least 4 off-street parking spaces for a house and its ADU together State floor Lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home must qualify Parking limit State law now caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Draft height The city draft allows newly eligible units up to 25 ft tall Planning Commission Recommended approval 6 to 0 on October 14, as reported Next step City Council vote expected in November 2026 Not confirmed: Whether the City Council has adopted the ordinance and its final standards. Ask Provo Planning for the current status. About Provo A college town where most households rent. Provo is the Utah County seat and home to Brigham Young University, with Provo Canyon to the east and Utah Lake to the west. A 2023 city report found about 59 percent of Pr"},{"t":"Sandy ADU Rules: Ordinance 26-55","u":"sandy-adu","k":"City","d":"Sandy adopted Ordinance 26-55, reportedly capping height near 18 ft and size at 2,000 sq ft. See what is confirmed and low-profile plans.","h":"Sandy now allows detached ADUs. | What we confirmed for Sandy | A south-valley city under the Wasatch with a split vote. | What this means for a Sandy homeowner. | A split council | Low height cap | A size cap, too | Salt Lake County market | Low-profile plans for an 18 foot cap. | Hillside and bench lots need a site plan first. | From a Sandy lot to move-in. | Sandy ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Sandy?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Sandy Sandy, Salt Lake County Sandy now allows detached ADUs. Sandy City adopted a detached ADU ordinance this fall to meet SB 284, after a lively council debate over size, height and parking. The details matter in Sandy, so a lot review before you design is worth it. Ordinance 26-55 adopted, fall 2026 Check my Sandy lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Sandy Source: Sandy City Long Range Planning Manager, email of November 10, 2026; Deseret News, October 14, 2026; local council coverage of Sandy City meetings; Utah Code 10-21-304. Official text: Sandy City Code, Chapter 21-11-1 (Extended Living Areas and ADUs) Adopted Ordinance 26-55, passed 4 to 2 as reported Height Reportedly capped at about 1.5 stories, around 18 ft Size Reportedly a 2,000 sq ft cap on gross ADU area Occupancy Owner occupancy and subordinate-unit rules are included Permit A detached ADU is a permitted use. An administrative special use permit confirms compliance and is approved when the standards are met, per Sandy planning State floor Lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home must qualify Parking State law caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Not confirmed: Sandy's final minimum lot size (the commission discussed 10,000 versus the state's 11,000 sq ft), and exact setbacks. Confirm with Sandy Planning. About Sandy A south-valley city un"},{"t":"Saratoga Springs ADUs: Ordinance 26-42","u":"saratoga-springs-adu","k":"City","d":"Saratoga Springs adopted Ordinance 26-42 on Sept. 15, 2026. See impact fees, HOA questions and which modular ADU plans fit.","h":"Saratoga Springs has opened the door to backyard ADUs. | What we confirmed for Saratoga Springs | A fast-growing lakeside city with a lot of HOAs. | What this means for a Saratoga Springs homeowner. | Rules now on the books | Impact fees can apply | Read your CC&Rs | More changes coming | Plans that suit a newer-subdivision lot. | Access, meters and capacity in Saratoga Springs. | From a Saratoga Springs lot to move-in. | Saratoga Springs ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Saratoga Springs?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Saratoga Springs Saratoga Springs, Utah County Saratoga Springs has opened the door to backyard ADUs. The Saratoga Springs City Council adopted its detached ADU rules on September 15, 2026, to comply with SB 284. Owners of qualifying lots can now plan a separate small home in the backyard, with local standards the city says it will keep refining. Ordinance 26-42 adopted, September 15, 2026 Check my Saratoga Springs lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Saratoga Springs Source: Saratoga Springs City Council minutes, September 15, 2026; Utah Code 10-21-304. Adopted September 15, 2026, Ordinance 26-42, 4 to 0 Lot Permitted on qualifying single-family lots of 11,000 sq ft or larger City may regulate Overall size, parking, design standards, rental restrictions and utility capacity Code layout One chapter with general standards, plus separate rules for internal and detached ADUs Impact fees The city may charge impact fees on detached ADUs, but not on internal ADUs Next steps The mayor said more refinement is coming over the next few months Not confirmed: The adopted size, height, setback and parking figures. Council members raised height, emergency access and utility capacity as open questions. About Saratoga Springs A fast-growing lakeside city with a lot of HOAs. Saratoga Springs spreads along the northwest shore of Utah Lake, with the Lake Mountain"},{"t":"Taylorsville ADUs: Ordinance 26-15 Adopted","u":"taylorsville-adu","k":"City","d":"Taylorsville adopted Ordinance 26-15 allowing detached ADUs on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more. See what changed, why utilities can cost 50 to 70 percent, and compact plans.","h":"Taylorsville now allows detached ADUs. | What we confirmed for Taylorsville | Established neighborhoods and a city looking ahead. | What this means for a Taylorsville homeowner. | Newly required | Budget the utilities | Convert what you have | Rental rules are inconsistent | Compact plans keep the utility run short. | The number Taylorsville staff told the council. | From a Taylorsville lot to move-in. | Taylorsville ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Taylorsville?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Taylorsville Taylorsville, Salt Lake County Taylorsville now allows detached ADUs. Detached ADUs were prohibited in Taylorsville, according to the city's own notice. On October 21, 2026 the City Council adopted Ordinance 26-15, a 5 to 0 vote, allowing them on residential lots of 11,000 square feet or more. Ordinance 26-15 adopted, October 21, 2026, 5 to 0 Check my Taylorsville lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Taylorsville Source: Taylorsville City Council meeting, October 21, 2026 (as published on OpenUtah); Taylorsville City Council minutes, October 7, 2026; Taylorsville City notice; Utah Code 10-21-304. Before Detached ADUs were prohibited, per the city's own notice Code today Covers internal ADUs and guesthouses State requirement Allow detached units on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home Conversions A process is required to convert legally built garages, sheds and barns Permits The state bars conditional use permits and limits parking and conflicting design standards Cost warning City staff said utility connections can run 50 to 70 percent of the cost of a detached unit Adopted Ordinance 26-15, City Council, October 21, 2026, 5 to 0 Eligible lots Residential lots of 11,000 sq ft or more, about 2,050 citywide per the city Not confirmed: The final setbacks, height step-back, plan review process and fees in the adopted t"},{"t":"Utah ADU Rules by City (SB 284)","u":"utah-adu-cities","k":"Cities","d":"Where Utah cities stand on detached backyard ADUs after SB 284: American Fork, Orem, Saratoga Springs, Lehi, Mapleton, Highland, Sandy, Taylorsville, West Valley City, Bountiful, Provo, Alpine, Vineyard, West Point, Layton, Lindon and Pleasant Grove.","h":"Which cities now allow a backyard ADU? | American Fork | Orem | Saratoga Springs | Lehi | Mapleton | Highland | Sandy | Taylorsville | West Valley City | Bountiful | Provo | Alpine | Vineyard","b":"Utah ADU rules by city Which cities now allow a backyard ADU? Utah's SB 284 required larger cities to allow detached ADUs by October 1, 2026. Here is where the cities we work in stand, based on council records and local reporting. Check my city and lot The SB 284 guide Adopted September 22, 2026 American Fork Utah County Not allowed before. Now permitted on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more, with a 24 ft height limit. See American Fork details Adopted September 2026 Orem Utah County Never allowed before. Now permitted, limited to a single story at 20 ft. See Orem details Adopted September 15, 2026 Saratoga Springs Utah County Ordinance 26-42 adopted 4 to 0. Impact fees can apply to detached units. See Saratoga Springs details Updated July 2026 Lehi Utah County Minimum lot cut from 14,520 sq ft to 11,000 sq ft. See Lehi details Code amended 2026 Mapleton Utah County Earlier code needed a half acre. Council amended it to comply with state law. See Mapleton details Adopted September 1, 2026 Highland Utah County Ordinance O-2026-28. One ADU per property, owner occupancy recorded on title. See Highland details Adopted fall 2026 Sandy Salt Lake County Ordinance 26-55. Permitted use with an administrative permit, per the city. See Sandy details Adopted October 21, 2026 Taylorsville Salt Lake County Detached ADUs were prohibited. Ordinance 26-15 adopted October 21, 2026. See Taylorsville de"},{"t":"Utah's Detached ADU Law (SB 284) Explained","u":"utah-adu-law-sb284","k":"Utah law","d":"What Utah SB 284 changed for detached ADUs: who it affects, the 11,000 square foot lot rule, what cities can still require, and how to check your lot.","h":"SB 284 made backyard ADUs a permitted use. | The short version | From \"ask your city\" to \"your city must say yes.\" | A permitted use | Capped parking | A path for existing buildings | Codes still apply | Homeowners in larger Utah cities. | Covered | Not required by this law | The law sets a floor, not a template. | Size and placement | Use and utilities | Five checks before you spend a dollar.","b":"Utah ADU law SB 284 made backyard ADUs a permitted use. Since October 1, 2026, larger Utah cities must allow a detached accessory dwelling unit (a separate small home in the backyard) on lots of 11,000 square feet or more. Here is who it affects, what cities can still require, and how to tell whether your lot qualifies. Find your city Check my lot The short version The rule: a covered city must allow one detached ADU on any lot of 11,000 square feet or larger that has a single-family home on it. The change: it is now a permitted use. In a primarily residential zone the city cannot require a conditional use permit. The catch: cities can still set size, height, setback, owner-occupancy and rental rules, so the details depend on your city. The source: Utah Code 10-21-304, enacted by S.B. 284 in the 2026 General Session. What changed From \"ask your city\" to \"your city must say yes.\" Before SB 284, whether you could build a separate backyard unit was up to each city, and many did not allow it. Internal ADUs, such as a basement apartment, were the common path. The new law requires covered cities to adopt a regulation that permits detached ADUs, and it limits what those regulations can do. A permitted use On a qualifying lot, a detached ADU is allowed as of right. In a primarily residential zone the city cannot require a conditional use permit, which removes a discretionary hearing fr"},{"t":"Vineyard ADUs: 12,000 vs 11,000 sq ft Lots","u":"vineyard-adu","k":"City","d":"Vineyard's published rule asked for 12,000 sq ft; SB 284 caps it at 11,000. See what is reported adopted and compact plans for Vineyard.","h":"Detached ADUs in Vineyard, and a lot-size change. | What we confirmed for Vineyard | A lakeshore city built on the old steel mill. | What this means for a Vineyard homeowner. | 12,000 down to 11,000 | Master-planned neighborhoods | Check the code section | We will verify for you | Small plans for smaller newer lots. | Newer lots, tight setbacks and HOA review. | From a Vineyard lot to move-in. | Vineyard ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in Vineyard?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / Vineyard Vineyard, Utah County Detached ADUs in Vineyard, and a lot-size change. Vineyard's published ADU rule asked for a detached unit lot of at least 12,000 square feet. State law now sets 11,000 square feet as the ceiling. Deseret News reports Vineyard among the cities that adopted compliant rules, so confirm the current text with the city. Reported adopted, confirm with the city Check my Vineyard lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for Vineyard Source: Vineyard City ADU guidance; Deseret News, October 14, 2026; Utah Code 10-21-304. Published rule Detached unit on a lot of at least 12,000 sq ft (VZC 15.34.060) State ceiling A city cannot require more than 11,000 sq ft for a detached ADU Reported Deseret News lists Vineyard among cities adopting compliant ordinances Permits No conditional use permit in a primarily residential zone Parking 1 space under 650 sq ft, 2 at 650 sq ft or more, at most Status We could not confirm the adopted text Not confirmed: The adopted lot-size wording, size caps, setbacks and parking. We saw the older 12,000 sq ft figure on the city's pages, so confirm what is current. About Vineyard A lakeshore city built on the old steel mill. Vineyard sits on the Utah Lake shoreline between Orem and Lindon, on land that was once part of the Geneva Steel site. It is one of the newest and fastest-changing cities in Utah County, w"},{"t":"West Point ADUs: Ordinance 569 and SB 284","u":"west-point-adu","k":"City","d":"West Point's council took up Ordinance 569 on accessory dwelling units Oct. 13, 2026. See today's rules and plans on a permanent foundation.","h":"West Point is updating its detached ADU rules. | What we confirmed for West Point | West Davis County, where the lots run level. | What this means for a West Point homeowner. | Permanent foundation | No basement | Owner occupancy | Davis County option | Single-story plans on a permanent foundation. | Flat lots, simpler site work. | From a West Point lot to move-in. | West Point ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in West Point?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / West Point West Point, Davis County West Point is updating its detached ADU rules. West Point's code allows an ADU only with an owner living on the property, and detached units have to meet specific standards. The City Council took up an ordinance on accessory dwelling units on October 13, 2026 as SB 284 requires. Ordinance on the October 13, 2026 council agenda Check my West Point lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for West Point Source: West Point City Council agenda, October 13, 2026; West Point City Code 17.70.060; Utah Code 10-21-304. Code today An ADU is allowed only where an owner-occupant lives on the property Detached standards A permanent foundation is required and basements are not allowed State requirement Allow a detached ADU on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home Council Ordinance 569 on accessory dwelling units was on the October 13 agenda Parking State law caps it at 1 space under 650 sq ft and 2 at 650 sq ft or more Status Outcome not confirmed Not confirmed: Whether the council adopted the ordinance, and its final lot-size, size and setback standards. Ask West Point for the current text. About West Point West Davis County, where the lots run level. West Point is in west Davis County, near Clinton, Syracuse and Layton, toward the Great Salt Lake. Much of the city sits on level ground with newer subdivisions, an"},{"t":"West Valley City ADUs: Ordinance 26-28 in Effect","u":"west-valley-city-adu","k":"City","d":"West Valley City adopted Ordinance 26-28, effective June 24, 2026, with detached ADU standards. See what the city confirmed, what SB 284 adds, and compact plans for your lot.","h":"West Valley City has detached ADU rules in effect. | What we confirmed for West Valley City | One of the valley's biggest cities, with every lot size. | What this means for a West Valley City homeowner. | State law applies now | Lot size decides it | The rules are on the books | We will check for you | Right-sized plans for an unknown rule set. | Check the lot before the plan. | From a West Valley City lot to move-in. | West Valley City ADU questions, answered. | Compare nearby cities. | Own a lot in West Valley City?","b":"Home / ADU rules by city / West Valley City West Valley City, Salt Lake County West Valley City has detached ADU rules in effect. West Valley City's Community Development Director confirmed in November 2026 that the City Council approved Ordinance 26-28 and that it took effect on June 24, 2026, with the detached ADU standards in the ordinance. Utah's SB 284 sets the state floor on top of that. Ordinance 26-28 adopted, effective June 24, 2026 Check my West Valley City lot ADU rules by city What we confirmed for West Valley City Source: West Valley City Community Development, email of November 10, 2026, attaching Ordinance 26-28; Utah Code 10-21-304, enacted by S.B. 284 (2026 General Session). Official text: West Valley City municipal code State requirement Allow a detached ADU on lots of 11,000 sq ft or more with a single-family home Permits No conditional use permit in a primarily residential zone Parking 1 space under 650 sq ft, 2 at 650 sq ft or more, at most Conversions A process to convert legally built accessory structures City rules Size, height, setbacks, owner occupancy and 90-day rental rules may apply Adopted Ordinance 26-28, effective June 24, 2026, per the city (detached ADU standards on pages 38 and 39 of the ordinance) Not confirmed: The specific size, height, setback and parking numbers in Ordinance 26-28. We have the ordinance from the city and are adding those "}];
